ammate great player  i'm so grateful that we drafted him that  that i get to play with him  and uh he's going to help our offense go  hopefully for a long time  kirk justin jefferson is a candidate for  offense of the rookie of the year and it  was just an amazing year  and he said he credited you a lot with  his success  in his first season but he also said you  take a lot more heat than you probably  deserve to take do you agree with that  assessment  you know what i i i know that the the  phrase that i've always kind of lived by  is people who know know and people who  don't  you know i'm not going to worry too much  about what they say but fortunately  through my journey whether it was high  school getting recruited  uh college you know where i got drafted  developing there  under kyle shanahan and sean mcvay and  jay gruden and others  people who don't know and that's really  been good enough for me it's given me a  great career and giving me great  opportunities  and to the people who don't really study  it and really try to dive into the  details  you know i'm not going to spend too much  time worrying about their opinion  because i i really want to value the  opinions of the people who really do  study  i know quarterbacks spend a lot of off  seasons working on specific things a lot  of them do do you do that how will you  spend this offseason is there something  specific that you're going to work on to  get better at  i just think longevity is such an  important piece of this  uh of this job you know being able to  stay healthy being able to come back  year after year and have your body feel  good in training camp and then all the  way through the season and so  even now in you know late january early  february my focus  is you know how can i you know have my  body at now turning 33 next summer  you know continue to perform at the  level it did when i was 23 24  and um you know tom brady's obviously  set a high bar drew brees much the same  way with how well they've played into  their early 40s mid 40s even  in tom's case and just trying to have  the right habits so with my  training the workouts diets sleep i'm  here with sleep number  uh this week doing media just want to  get the word out about  you know the importance of quality sleep  and sleep number 360 smart bed that i  use and the sleep iq score that their  sleep number app provides for me  the difference it makes and and staying  healthy and having a long career so  that's really something that i try to  take seriously not only sleeping well in  season but also in the off season so i  can  hopefully play as long as a guy like  drew or tom  and you were able to make that very  seamless and professional transition to  the pitch  because you sleep on a sleep number bed  see that's how it works  the other guys can't do it the other  guys can't do it that way because  they're on a different kind of a bed  hey kirk about the off season because  we've heard different things about  different quarterbacks how much they  throw  how much do you actually pick up a  football throw a football  during that dead spot in the off season  well one off season i chose not to throw  i shut it down for about  two to three months and when i picked  the football back up in  whatever it was mid to late march i  didn't like how i felt and so i decided  from then on i'm gonna always throw  i remember when i was in washington i  got to meet max scherzer and i talked to  him a little bit about arm maintenance  and his  arm care that he's learned as a pitcher  and he said he rarely ever goes  a week without throwing a baseball even  in the off season if he goes to  fiji and goes snorkeling or scuba diving  he still brings a baseball and a glove  with him and makes sure that he throws  so  i i've kind of taken that same approach  i like to stay thrown and so  even the last few days i'll grab my  brother down the street we'll go outside  and play catch just to make sure that  i'm always throwing  and uh and i think that's a big piece of  keeping my shoulder healthy and strong  as  we get older and move forward well with  your brother is it like the old days you  go out in the street or do you own the  backyard  it's absolutely like the old days uh  although this now he's got gloves i  don't really make him catch without  gloves but uh  it's just straight in the backyard  sometimes in the driveway and again i  don't need it to be fancy i just say hey  i just need to get some throws in and  he's got good enough hands he's a good  enough athlete where i can rifle it at  him and it works for me  i'm just curious kirk you've been where  dak prescott is right now i don't know  if you've  talked to dak and given him any advice  but if if you have great but if you  haven't what advice would you give him  well the only piece i've said to him and  crossing his path that going back maybe  a couple off seasons was just to make  the point that the franchise tag  can be your friend you know it can be a  help to you if  if that's the route you choose to go um  and so you know he played on the  franchise tag this past year  i think he's in a great spot now  everybody knows he can play that he's an  elite quarterback  and um you know really the ball is more  in the in the court of  the cowboys and what they want to do  going forward but uh  i think it's a no-brainer that dax the  real deal and is going to have a great  career moving forward and  um you know he's in a really good spot  what do you think you need to do kirk to  get to that next level i know  two or three years ago you said i don't  want to be known as a 500 quarterback  to take that next step to get to a super  bowl and win a super bowl  what do you need to do well you just got  to win games there's no secret formula  you know if it was hey  i've got to complete a higher percentage  of my passes on third down  then you know you'd suddenly do that and  and you'd win i think it's just so many  variables t